=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
No fault exists in this network. The reported helpdesk complaint — that
"web1.local" is completely down for users on host_1_1_1_1 — does not
correspond to any actual failure in the lab.

A full end-to-end verification was performed: DNS resolution of web1.local
on host_1_1_1_1 returns the correct address (10.200.0.4, web_server_1).
ICMP ping from host_1_1_1_1 to web_server_1 completes with 0% packet loss
across all four hops (switch_dist_1_1 → router_core_1 → router_core_3 →
switch_server_access → web_server_1). An HTTP GET to http://web1.local/
from host_1_1_1_1 returns HTTP 200 OK with the correct page content
("Welcome to Web Server 1"). Apache2 is running on web_server_1, bound to
0.0.0.0:80, with no error log entries. All iptables chains on every transit
device (router_core_1, router_core_3, switch_server_access) are empty with
default ACCEPT policy. The OSPF fabric is fully converged — all
router_core_1 neighbors (router_core_2, router_core_3, switch_dist_1_1)
are in Full/DR state, and switch_server_access correctly reaches
router_core_3 in Full/Backup state. There are no misconfigured or missing
static routes. Connectivity from host_2_1_1_1 is equally healthy.

The ticket's premise is false. The web portal is fully operational. The
correct action is to close the ticket as a false alarm and verify with the
reporting users whether the issue was transient, occurred on a different
service, or was a client-side browser/cache problem.
=== END ===
